62
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:39,080
And so the class here is--

63
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:44,480
so the two key topics
for this class is--

64
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:54,820
so key topics, one
is you will learn how

65
00:04:54,820 --> 00:04:59,150
to write a mathematical proof.

66
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:09,080
So what does it mean
to write something

67
00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:12,380
so that is
mathematically rigorous?

68
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:14,220
And the second thing
that you will learn

69
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:15,630
is how to prove theorems.

70
00:05:25,820 --> 00:05:29,940
And so here's an
example of this.

71
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:32,660
Let's suppose you
have a function.

72
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:34,440
So suppose you
have some interval.

73
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:38,380
So a and b are real numbers.

74
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:44,740
And you have a function f
defined on this interval.

75
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:47,140
And you take real values.

76
00:05:47,140 --> 00:05:49,800
And so here, you have
the interval here, a.

77
00:05:49,800 --> 00:05:52,480
Here's b.

78
00:05:52,480 --> 00:05:58,620
And let's say that the
value at a is negative,

79
00:05:58,620 --> 00:06:03,600
and the value at the other
endpoint, b, is positive.

80
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:08,280
So if you were drawing the
graph, here, you have a, maybe.

81
00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:09,920
Here, you have b.

82
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:13,160
And then the function
initially is negative,

83
00:06:13,160 --> 00:06:15,300
and it end up being positive.

84
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:17,360
So this is f of a.

85
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:20,840
Here, you have f of b.

86
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:23,420
And then this function
here is continuous.

87
00:06:28,440 --> 00:06:31,100
So the function looks
maybe like this.

88
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:36,500
So f is continuous.

89
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:39,050
So the intermediate
value theorem's saying--

90
00:06:54,850 --> 00:06:56,780
so the intermediate
value theorem.

91
00:07:09,867 --> 00:07:11,450
It's saying the
following, that if you

92
00:07:11,450 --> 00:07:15,130
have a continuous function
on an interval, then

93
00:07:15,130 --> 00:07:18,650
it takes all values in between.

94
00:07:18,650 --> 00:07:21,570
So the intermediate value
theorem says in particular

95
00:07:21,570 --> 00:07:27,810
that there exists a c.

96
00:07:27,810 --> 00:07:32,930
And the c here is
between a and b

97
00:07:32,930 --> 00:07:38,190
so that the value of
the function at c is 0.

98
00:07:38,190 --> 00:07:42,330
Because the function starts off
negative, and it end positive.

99
00:07:42,330 --> 00:07:45,810
So there must be somewhere
in between where it hits 0.

100
00:07:45,810 --> 00:07:49,150
That's the intermediate
value theorem.

101
00:07:49,150 --> 00:07:55,390
But now the question is then,
how do we make this into--

102
00:07:55,390 --> 00:07:57,470
and it's kind of obvious.

103
00:07:57,470 --> 00:08:04,790
It's sort of intuitively obvious
that if you take a function,

104
00:08:04,790 --> 00:08:08,590
continuous means something
like that if you were drawing

105
00:08:08,590 --> 00:08:13,550
the graph, and you'd have
this piece of chalk here,

106
00:08:13,550 --> 00:08:17,110
then as you're
drawing the graph,

107
00:08:17,110 --> 00:08:20,690
the chalk is not allowed
to leave your table.

108
00:08:20,690 --> 00:08:24,030
It can't have a
function like this here

109
00:08:24,030 --> 00:08:28,210
that jumps, so go like this,
and then jumps like this.

110
00:08:28,210 --> 00:08:30,330
That's not continuous.

111
00:08:30,330 --> 00:08:34,030
This is not continuous.

112
00:08:34,030 --> 00:08:38,730
So continuous function is that
you have to, when you draw it,

113
00:08:38,730 --> 00:08:40,470
it's in one motion.

114
00:08:40,470 --> 00:08:44,130
The chalk doesn't
leave the blackboard.

115
00:08:44,130 --> 00:08:47,490
And so it seems
obvious that there

116
00:08:47,490 --> 00:08:51,130
should be some point
in between where

117
00:08:51,130 --> 00:08:53,530
you're crossing this axis here.

118
00:08:53,530 --> 00:08:56,070
But how do we make this
into a rigorous proof?

119
00:08:56,070 --> 00:08:58,510
And what is the property?

120
00:08:58,510 --> 00:09:00,440
So how do we make
this into a proof?

121
00:09:04,290 --> 00:09:20,950
So how do we make this
intuition into a proof?

122
00:09:25,210 --> 00:09:29,350
And this is not just
a semantic question.

123
00:09:29,350 --> 00:09:32,450
It's not just a crazy question.

124
00:09:32,450 --> 00:09:35,730
Maybe in this case,
it seemed clear.

125
00:09:35,730 --> 00:09:39,850
But very quickly, if
your foundation is not

126
00:09:39,850 --> 00:09:43,110
totally rigorous, then
you get into something

127
00:09:43,110 --> 00:09:47,510
where it seems to be the case,
but it might not be the case.

128
00:09:47,510 --> 00:09:50,910
And it's also very
difficult to then--

129
00:09:50,910 --> 00:09:52,590
quickly, that's
where you arrive.

130
00:09:52,590 --> 00:09:54,690
And you also quickly
arrive to that

131
00:09:54,690 --> 00:09:59,090
you don't even know whether
it should be the case or not.

132
00:09:59,090 --> 00:10:03,610
And so it's really important to
build things up, step to step,

133
00:10:03,610 --> 00:10:06,310
where each step is
totally rigorous.

134
00:10:06,310 --> 00:10:10,930
And you write it
because it's not only

135
00:10:10,930 --> 00:10:13,850
you know for sure
that things are right,

136
00:10:13,850 --> 00:10:18,830
but it also makes it much easier
to predict what should be true.

137
00:10:18,830 --> 00:10:24,150
And so in order
to understand why

138
00:10:24,150 --> 00:10:30,230
there should be some
c between a and b

139
00:10:30,230 --> 00:10:36,190
where the value is 0,
immediately, the first question

140
00:10:36,190 --> 00:10:37,550
that arises--

141
00:10:37,550 --> 00:10:38,730
what are you using?

142
00:10:38,730 --> 00:10:43,110
What property do we need
to make this into a proof?

143
00:10:43,110 --> 00:10:45,970
What property do we need
of the real numbers?

144
00:10:45,970 --> 00:10:49,830
So this is the first
question that arises.

145
00:10:49,830 --> 00:11:05,940
So what property are we
using of the real numbers?

146
00:11:12,810 --> 00:11:18,050
And so, in fact, what
is the real numbers?

147
00:11:18,050 --> 00:11:19,640
So what are the real numbers?

148
00:11:33,770 --> 00:11:41,098
And so the thing is
that, in particular,

149
00:11:41,098 --> 00:11:43,990
what is square root of 2?

150
00:11:43,990 --> 00:11:45,690
So what is square root of 2?

151
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:55,520
And is that a real number?

152
00:11:58,270 --> 00:12:00,850
Is square root of
2 a real number?

153
00:12:13,070 --> 00:12:15,870
So those are some
of the questions

154
00:12:15,870 --> 00:12:19,670
that you immediately get to.

155
00:12:19,670 --> 00:12:24,910
And so now-- but so
the answer to, what

156
00:12:24,910 --> 00:12:27,730
are the properties that you're
using of the real numbers?

157
00:12:27,730 --> 00:12:33,810
So the answer to this question
here is the following.

158
00:12:43,050 --> 00:12:45,110
So the answer to
this question is--

159
00:12:45,110 --> 00:12:50,130
so what are the property of the
real numbers that you're using?

160
00:12:50,130 --> 00:12:59,850
So the answer to that
is the following,

161
00:12:59,850 --> 00:13:17,322
that the real numbers-- so R
is a complete ordered field

162
00:13:17,322 --> 00:13:39,430
that contains the rational
numbers, the rational numbers,

163
00:13:39,430 --> 00:13:40,430
Q.

164
00:13:40,430 --> 00:13:43,790
I denote the rational numbers
by Q and the real numbers

165
00:13:43,790 --> 00:13:54,110
by capital R. So in order to
make this the intermediate value

166
00:13:54,110 --> 00:13:58,670
theorem, this property that
if you have an integral from a

167
00:13:58,670 --> 00:14:02,290
to b, you have a continuous
function on that interval.

168
00:14:02,290 --> 00:14:04,390
And it starts off
negative, and it

169
00:14:04,390 --> 00:14:07,830
ends positive-- that then
there is some value in between,

170
00:14:07,830 --> 00:14:09,130
where 0 is achieved.

171
00:14:09,130 --> 00:14:12,390
In order to make that
rigorous, what you're

172
00:14:12,390 --> 00:14:15,230
using about the real numbers
is that it's complete

173
00:14:15,230 --> 00:14:18,430
ordered field that contains
irrational numbers.

174
00:14:18,430 --> 00:14:21,430
So we need to understand
what this here means.

175
00:14:21,430 --> 00:14:27,070
And in this lecture here, I
will not explain complete.

176
00:14:27,070 --> 00:14:29,430
I will do that at
a later lecture.

177
00:14:29,430 --> 00:14:35,050
But we will understand the rest
of what are these properties

178
00:14:35,050 --> 00:14:36,570
that we're using.

179
00:14:36,570 --> 00:14:38,470
So just first, some notation--

180
00:14:56,570 --> 00:15:00,890
so the natural numbers
here, this here

181
00:15:00,890 --> 00:15:08,610
is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, et cetera.

182
00:15:08,610 --> 00:15:10,220
So these are the
natural numbers.

183
00:15:21,050 --> 00:15:27,340
The integers-- so 2 set
here, these are the integers.

184
00:15:31,590 --> 00:15:36,490
And the integers are
simply the natural numbers.

185
00:15:36,490 --> 00:15:39,910
But then it also allows
them to be negative,

186
00:15:39,910 --> 00:15:41,910
and it also includes 0.

187
00:15:41,910 --> 00:15:47,710
So this is 0 plus or
minus 1, plus or minus 2,

188
00:15:47,710 --> 00:15:51,430
plus or minus 3, et cetera.

189
00:15:51,430 --> 00:15:54,110
These are the integers.

190
00:15:54,110 --> 00:16:05,230
The rational numbers are then--
so the rational numbers, Q,

191
00:16:05,230 --> 00:16:10,810
so these are numbers
of the form m/n,

192
00:16:10,810 --> 00:16:17,550
where m here is an
integer and n here--

193
00:16:17,550 --> 00:16:18,400
sorry.

194
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:22,230
Yeah, m is an integer, and
n here is a natural number.

195
00:16:24,830 --> 00:16:27,950
That's a rational number.

196
00:16:27,950 --> 00:16:32,490
And so now what
are the properties?

197
00:16:32,490 --> 00:16:36,090
So we're building up to trying
to understand what property

198
00:16:36,090 --> 00:16:37,530
of the real numbers is.

199
00:16:37,530 --> 00:16:42,370
But let's try to somehow
understand it a bit better.

200
00:16:42,370 --> 00:16:44,610
What are the properties
that we really have

201
00:16:44,610 --> 00:16:47,810
about the rational numbers?

202
00:16:47,810 --> 00:16:51,370
So for rational
numbers, we have--

203
00:16:51,370 --> 00:16:56,956
so we write a rational number
as a quotient, like that.

204
00:16:56,956 --> 00:16:59,050
But of course,
there are many ways

205
00:16:59,050 --> 00:17:01,370
that you can write a quotient.

206
00:17:01,370 --> 00:17:04,040
So properties of the
rational numbers--

207
00:17:16,609 --> 00:17:21,569
so we write it as a
quotient, like this.

208
00:17:21,569 --> 00:17:26,290
But this is not
uniquely defined.

209
00:17:26,290 --> 00:17:29,670
If you take a
quotient, like this,

210
00:17:29,670 --> 00:17:35,875
you think that this here is
equal to another quotient,

211
00:17:35,875 --> 00:17:38,430
right?

212
00:17:38,430 --> 00:17:47,390
Again, m1 and m2 are
integers, and n1 and n2

213
00:17:47,390 --> 00:17:50,110
are natural numbers.

214
00:17:50,110 --> 00:17:53,830
Two quotients are
the same if and only

215
00:17:53,830 --> 00:18:00,310
if that if you multiply this
over here and this over there,

216
00:18:00,310 --> 00:18:02,970
then those numbers are the same.

217
00:18:02,970 --> 00:18:11,670
So n2 times N2 is
equal to N2 times n1.

218
00:18:11,670 --> 00:18:15,030
Those two rational
numbers are the same if

219
00:18:15,030 --> 00:18:19,230
and only if those two natural
numbers are the same--

220
00:18:19,230 --> 00:18:23,176
or integers, because
m could be negative.

221
00:18:27,050 --> 00:18:33,450
So again, a rational number, it
has a representative like this.

222
00:18:33,450 --> 00:18:37,130
But you could think
about it as always, you

223
00:18:37,130 --> 00:18:39,270
can write this quotient.

224
00:18:39,270 --> 00:18:41,970
And two quotients are
the same if and only

225
00:18:41,970 --> 00:18:45,210
if this here is the case.

226
00:18:45,210 --> 00:18:48,280
So this is what it means
to be a rational number.

227
00:18:53,205 --> 00:18:56,770
But then there are
some operations.

228
00:18:56,770 --> 00:18:59,510
If you have two
rational numbers,

229
00:18:59,510 --> 00:19:02,200
then you can add two
rational numbers together.

230
00:19:05,630 --> 00:19:09,570
And that's really important,
that you have some operations

231
00:19:09,570 --> 00:19:12,370
on the rational numbers.

232
00:19:12,370 --> 00:19:14,030
So if you take--

233
00:19:18,130 --> 00:19:21,650
this here is one
rational number.

234
00:19:21,650 --> 00:19:25,650
And if you now take another
rational number, m2/n2--

235
00:19:29,910 --> 00:19:34,196
then how do you add two
rational numbers together?

236
00:19:34,196 --> 00:19:36,750
Well, of course, you
know that you put it

237
00:19:36,750 --> 00:19:38,630
on the common denominator.

238
00:19:38,630 --> 00:19:49,430
So this here is m1 times n2 plus
m2 times n1 divided by n1 times

239
00:19:49,430 --> 00:19:52,270
n2.

240
00:19:52,270 --> 00:19:55,310
This here is an integer
in the denominator.

241
00:19:55,310 --> 00:19:58,970
And this is a natural
number in the denominator.

242
00:19:58,970 --> 00:20:03,990
And so that's representative
of a rational number.

243
00:20:03,990 --> 00:20:06,950
So that's an operation.

244
00:20:06,950 --> 00:20:12,170
Now, in order to really
know that this is--

245
00:20:12,170 --> 00:20:17,350
so the idea here is that if
you take one rational number,

246
00:20:17,350 --> 00:20:20,910
like this, and you take
another rational number,

247
00:20:20,910 --> 00:20:25,240
like that, then
you can add them.

248
00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:30,096
And that gives you
another rational number.

249
00:20:30,096 --> 00:20:35,120
That's this operation that we
know so well and its addition.

250
00:20:38,240 --> 00:20:41,440
In order for this
really to make sense,

251
00:20:41,440 --> 00:20:48,680
you should check that this
rational number you get

252
00:20:48,680 --> 00:20:52,200
is independent on
the representation.

253
00:20:52,200 --> 00:20:54,680
If you have another
representation

254
00:20:54,680 --> 00:20:59,240
for the same rational number
and another representation

255
00:20:59,240 --> 00:21:02,560
for the same rational number,
then when you add them,

256
00:21:02,560 --> 00:21:04,920
you should get
another representation

257
00:21:04,920 --> 00:21:08,200
for the same rational number.

258
00:21:11,200 --> 00:21:15,560
And we will check one of
these things in just a minute.

259
00:21:15,560 --> 00:21:19,710
The other thing is that--

260
00:21:22,740 --> 00:21:24,580
so that was one operation.

261
00:21:24,580 --> 00:21:27,580
When you have the
rational number,

262
00:21:27,580 --> 00:21:30,620
then addition is one operation.

263
00:21:30,620 --> 00:21:33,880
Another operation is, of
course, multiplication.

264
00:21:37,260 --> 00:21:39,760
So another operation
is multiplication.

265
00:21:49,260 --> 00:21:53,740
And so if you take one
rational number, like this,

266
00:21:53,740 --> 00:21:57,700
and you take another
rational number,

267
00:21:57,700 --> 00:22:01,460
like that, then if
you multiply them

268
00:22:01,460 --> 00:22:04,340
together-- so what does
it mean to multiply

269
00:22:04,340 --> 00:22:06,540
two rational number together?

270
00:22:06,540 --> 00:22:09,320
Well, of course, you
know this very well.

271
00:22:09,320 --> 00:22:13,420
It's just multiplying
the denominator.

272
00:22:13,420 --> 00:22:15,940
So that gives you an integer.

273
00:22:15,940 --> 00:22:18,760
And then multiplying
the denominator,

274
00:22:18,760 --> 00:22:20,260
that gives you a natural number.

275
00:22:20,260 --> 00:22:22,060
So this here is a
rational number.

276
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:29,180
And then there's another thing.

277
00:22:29,180 --> 00:22:31,760
So this is addition.

278
00:22:31,760 --> 00:22:32,900
This was addition.

279
00:22:32,900 --> 00:22:34,880
This is multiplication.

280
00:22:34,880 --> 00:22:38,600
But there's another
important property

281
00:22:38,600 --> 00:22:41,040
that you have for
the rational number.

282
00:22:41,040 --> 00:22:42,350
And that is an ordering.

283
00:22:46,640 --> 00:22:48,920
Again, we're trying to
understand this question

284
00:22:48,920 --> 00:22:49,700
up there.

285
00:22:49,700 --> 00:22:52,360
But we want to try to
understand, how do we

286
00:22:52,360 --> 00:22:55,660
make the intermediate
value theorem--

287
00:22:55,660 --> 00:22:57,480
how do we make this rigorous?

288
00:22:57,480 --> 00:22:59,800
And so we need to
understand what property

289
00:22:59,800 --> 00:23:03,360
of the real numbers we need.

290
00:23:03,360 --> 00:23:05,260
And so this is
explained in this.

291
00:23:05,260 --> 00:23:08,080
And we were working towards
trying to understand

292
00:23:08,080 --> 00:23:10,060
the different concepts in this.

293
00:23:10,060 --> 00:23:10,940
What is a field?

294
00:23:10,940 --> 00:23:13,960
That's what we're
trying to understand.

295
00:23:13,960 --> 00:23:17,960
And so we have addition
in the rational numbers.

296
00:23:17,960 --> 00:23:19,000
We have addition.

297
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:20,420
We have multiplication.

298
00:23:20,420 --> 00:23:24,900
But we have another operation
on it, which is an ordering.

299
00:23:24,900 --> 00:23:31,340
So if you take a
rational number here,

300
00:23:31,340 --> 00:23:34,380
then you can say that this
rational number is less

301
00:23:34,380 --> 00:23:36,460
than another rational number.

302
00:23:36,460 --> 00:23:40,420
When is it less than
another rational number,

303
00:23:40,420 --> 00:23:42,620
this is equivalent to saying--

304
00:23:42,620 --> 00:23:46,680
remember that these here
are natural numbers.

305
00:23:46,680 --> 00:23:48,140
So they're positive.

306
00:23:48,140 --> 00:23:51,420
So that this rational
number is less

307
00:23:51,420 --> 00:23:55,100
than that just
means that m1 times

308
00:23:55,100 --> 00:23:59,120
n2 is less than m2 times n1.

309
00:24:01,820 --> 00:24:07,160
Here, I am just multiplying
over here over here.

310
00:24:07,160 --> 00:24:12,180
And I'm using, of course, that
these numbers here are positive.

311
00:24:12,180 --> 00:24:12,860
OK.

312
00:24:12,860 --> 00:24:14,320
So this is an ordering.

313
00:24:14,320 --> 00:24:18,740
So in the rational numbers,
there is actually ordering.

314
00:24:18,740 --> 00:24:21,660
So that's really important also.

315
00:24:21,660 --> 00:24:24,860
So we have these two operations,
and then we have an ordering.

316
00:24:31,460 --> 00:24:34,180
So here is-- so
let me just check.

317
00:24:34,180 --> 00:24:38,840
So I said that when we
talked about addition, when

318
00:24:38,840 --> 00:24:42,000
we talk about addition here,
then there was something,

319
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:46,620
really, you had to check,
that this even made any sense.

320
00:24:46,620 --> 00:24:49,480
And for this to
make any sense means

321
00:24:49,480 --> 00:24:54,720
that this rational number
you get from adding these two

322
00:24:54,720 --> 00:24:58,760
is independent on how you
write this rational number

323
00:24:58,760 --> 00:25:01,080
and how you write
that rational number--

324
00:25:01,080 --> 00:25:04,960
and the same for multiplication.

325
00:25:04,960 --> 00:25:07,030
So let us check that.

326
00:25:12,100 --> 00:25:29,372
So this here is checking
that multiplication

327
00:25:29,372 --> 00:25:46,860
is a well-defined operation,
i.e, it is independent.

328
00:25:46,860 --> 00:25:57,040
It is independent of
the representative.

329
00:26:04,220 --> 00:26:09,440
So we are multiplying-- we
take this rational number.

330
00:26:09,440 --> 00:26:11,920
Sorry, let me write
a little bit bigger--

331
00:26:11,920 --> 00:26:13,840
this rational number.

332
00:26:13,840 --> 00:26:17,480
So this is one rational number.

333
00:26:17,480 --> 00:26:24,280
And we multiply
this rational number

334
00:26:24,280 --> 00:26:26,300
by another rational number.

335
00:26:26,300 --> 00:26:29,690
Let's call it p1 comma q1.

336
00:26:33,280 --> 00:26:35,800
And then we take
another representative

337
00:26:35,800 --> 00:26:38,040
for the same rational number.

338
00:26:38,040 --> 00:26:39,500
And let's call this m2/n2.

339
00:26:42,840 --> 00:26:46,780
And for this rational number,
we take another representative.

340
00:26:51,040 --> 00:26:56,240
And we want to prove
that this number here is

341
00:26:56,240 --> 00:27:00,280
the same as that number here.

342
00:27:00,280 --> 00:27:01,970
So how do we do that?

343
00:27:08,560 --> 00:27:13,920
So we have that this here
and this here is the same--

344
00:27:13,920 --> 00:27:15,460
it's two different
representation

345
00:27:15,460 --> 00:27:17,420
of the same rational number.

346
00:27:17,420 --> 00:27:26,460
So we have that m1/n1
is equal to m2/n2.

347
00:27:26,460 --> 00:27:31,900
So remember that this just meant
that when you multiply over

348
00:27:31,900 --> 00:27:35,140
here, like that, then you
get the same integers-- so

349
00:27:35,140 --> 00:27:40,040
this here, n2, plus m2 times n1.

350
00:27:40,040 --> 00:27:43,500
That's what it meant, that
these here were the same.

351
00:27:43,500 --> 00:27:48,620
And likewise, you have the
two rational numbers here.

352
00:27:48,620 --> 00:27:49,620
This is one.

353
00:27:49,620 --> 00:27:54,820
This is equal to that
p2 divided by q2.

354
00:27:54,820 --> 00:27:57,900
This just meant that
when you multiply over,

355
00:27:57,900 --> 00:28:03,750
p1 q2 is equal to p2 q1.

356
00:28:07,480 --> 00:28:12,000
So this just meant that
these two represent

357
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:13,780
the same rational number.

358
00:28:13,780 --> 00:28:17,560
These two represent the
same rational number.

359
00:28:17,560 --> 00:28:21,920
And now what we need to prove
is that the product here

360
00:28:21,920 --> 00:28:26,480
is independent of
these representations.

361
00:28:26,480 --> 00:28:30,160
So the product here is--

362
00:28:30,160 --> 00:28:33,420
so we need to prove the
products of the first--

363
00:28:36,520 --> 00:28:40,320
this product here,
this product here,

364
00:28:40,320 --> 00:28:50,040
this is just m1 times p1
divided by n1 times q1.

365
00:28:50,040 --> 00:28:59,960
And this is what we want to
show, that this thing here

366
00:28:59,960 --> 00:29:03,720
is the same if we took
this other representation

367
00:29:03,720 --> 00:29:07,340
and multiply it by the
other reputation of this.

368
00:29:07,340 --> 00:29:15,900
So this is m2 times
p2, m2 times q2.

369
00:29:15,900 --> 00:29:19,980
That's what we want to prove,
that those two are same.

370
00:29:19,980 --> 00:29:22,660
And so we need to prove that.

371
00:29:22,660 --> 00:29:35,420
So we need to show
that m1 p1 times

372
00:29:35,420 --> 00:29:40,940
this denominator here into q2--

373
00:29:40,940 --> 00:29:50,220
we need to prove, need to
show that this thing here

374
00:29:50,220 --> 00:29:58,920
is equal to m2 p2 n1 q1.

375
00:29:58,920 --> 00:30:02,420
That's what we need to prove.

376
00:30:02,420 --> 00:30:04,860
But now let's see.

377
00:30:04,860 --> 00:30:20,640
So we know that if we take
m1 and together with n2,

378
00:30:20,640 --> 00:30:25,620
m1 together with n2,
this is the same as--

379
00:30:25,620 --> 00:30:32,340
so this here is
the same as m2 n1.

380
00:30:32,340 --> 00:30:35,600
This is what this here say.

381
00:30:35,600 --> 00:30:40,760
And it also says that if you
take p1 together with q2, then

382
00:30:40,760 --> 00:30:44,120
you may as well write
this thing here.

383
00:30:44,120 --> 00:30:49,400
So you may as well write p2 q1.

384
00:30:49,400 --> 00:30:52,400
So this thing here
is equal to that.

385
00:30:52,400 --> 00:30:56,360
But now you see that
here, you have m2.

386
00:30:56,360 --> 00:30:57,920
Here, you have n1.

387
00:30:57,920 --> 00:30:59,440
Here, you have p2.

388
00:30:59,440 --> 00:31:01,100
Here, you have q1.

389
00:31:01,100 --> 00:31:03,140
So those two are the same.

390
00:31:03,140 --> 00:31:07,780
So this proves that this
operation of multiplication

391
00:31:07,780 --> 00:31:10,340
is well defined in
the rational number--

392
00:31:10,340 --> 00:31:13,940
and similarly for addition.

393
00:31:13,940 --> 00:31:16,460
The reason, of course,
why I chose multiplication

394
00:31:16,460 --> 00:31:20,160
is that it looks a little
simpler on the blackboard.

395
00:31:20,160 --> 00:31:22,760
It's a little more messy
if you did addition.

396
00:31:22,760 --> 00:31:27,212
But it's just exactly the same.

397
00:31:27,212 --> 00:31:27,712
OK.

398
00:31:36,660 --> 00:31:40,650
So this is also
supposed to illustrate

399
00:31:40,650 --> 00:31:42,800
how detailed a proof is.

400
00:31:42,800 --> 00:31:46,260
So when we have a
concept like this,

401
00:31:46,260 --> 00:31:50,280
we want to make sure that
it really makes sense.

402
00:31:50,280 --> 00:31:53,160
And in this case, it makes
sense that it's well defined.

403
00:31:53,160 --> 00:31:56,500
It's independent
under representation.

404
00:31:56,500 --> 00:31:59,170
And so the proof here, this
here would be the proof.

405
00:32:02,793 --> 00:32:03,293
OK.

406
00:32:05,880 --> 00:32:08,320
So the takeaway
here at the moment

407
00:32:08,320 --> 00:32:10,860
is, we're building towards this.

408
00:32:10,860 --> 00:32:12,220
We're understanding a field.

409
00:32:12,220 --> 00:32:16,200
We want to understand
the real numbers.

410
00:32:16,200 --> 00:32:22,160
But the takeaway at the moment
is that on the rational numbers,

411
00:32:22,160 --> 00:32:25,400
there is a way of adding
two rational numbers

412
00:32:25,400 --> 00:32:27,540
and multiplying two
rational numbers.

413
00:32:27,540 --> 00:32:31,080
And there's also an ordering.

414
00:32:31,080 --> 00:32:36,740
And so this leads us to
the following definition.

415
00:32:47,680 --> 00:32:50,960
And so this is the
definition of a field.

416
00:32:50,960 --> 00:33:05,050
So a field-- and I will use F
here, capital F for a field--

417
00:33:05,050 --> 00:33:06,070
is the following.

418
00:33:12,290 --> 00:33:18,750
So it's a set with
two operations.

419
00:33:26,450 --> 00:33:31,530
And the operations we are
denoting suggestively by--

420
00:33:31,530 --> 00:33:34,410
so the two operations
are denoted suggestively

421
00:33:34,410 --> 00:33:49,005
by a plus and a
multiplication, like a dot.

422
00:33:49,005 --> 00:33:52,150
But this is just abstract.

423
00:33:52,150 --> 00:33:56,750
So we have a set, and we
have these two operations.

424
00:33:56,750 --> 00:34:01,470
What property do these two
operations need to have?

425
00:34:01,470 --> 00:34:05,790
Each of them have
five properties.

426
00:34:05,790 --> 00:34:08,750
So that means that
there are 10 properties.

427
00:34:08,750 --> 00:34:12,350
And then there's 11 properties
that chain those two operations

428
00:34:12,350 --> 00:34:13,110
together.

429
00:34:13,110 --> 00:34:15,530
So let's start with addition.

430
00:34:15,530 --> 00:34:21,170
So properties of addition--

431
00:34:25,909 --> 00:34:30,449
so the first property is
that addition make any sense.

432
00:34:30,449 --> 00:34:35,590
So if you take a
element in the field

433
00:34:35,590 --> 00:34:40,270
and you take another
element in the field,

434
00:34:40,270 --> 00:34:44,969
then addition gives
you a third element.

435
00:34:44,969 --> 00:34:47,230
So when you add
these together, this

436
00:34:47,230 --> 00:34:51,790
is just a third
element in the field.

437
00:34:51,790 --> 00:34:54,050
So this is the first
property, that it really

438
00:34:54,050 --> 00:34:57,730
is an operation that when you
take two elements of the field,

439
00:34:57,730 --> 00:35:01,290
then you get another
element in the field.

440
00:35:01,290 --> 00:35:03,290
So this is one.

441
00:35:03,290 --> 00:35:10,450
The second is that if you
add two elements together--

442
00:35:10,450 --> 00:35:12,830
so x and y are in the field.

443
00:35:12,830 --> 00:35:15,190
If you add two
elements together,

444
00:35:15,190 --> 00:35:20,350
then the ordering that
you're adding is irrelevant.

445
00:35:20,350 --> 00:35:23,390
So this here is the
same as y plus x.

446
00:35:28,530 --> 00:35:32,410
Then the next one is--
this is sometimes called

447
00:35:32,410 --> 00:35:35,890
abelian or commutative,
this property here.

448
00:35:35,890 --> 00:35:38,890
Then there's a third
property, which

449
00:35:38,890 --> 00:35:42,810
is that if you add
two elements together

450
00:35:42,810 --> 00:35:48,770
and then you add a third
element to the sum of these two,

451
00:35:48,770 --> 00:35:52,830
then this here is
the same as that you

452
00:35:52,830 --> 00:35:58,150
can add the last
two first and then

453
00:35:58,150 --> 00:36:01,010
add the first one to the
sum of the other two.

454
00:36:17,790 --> 00:36:23,670
And so the fourth
property is that there

455
00:36:23,670 --> 00:36:28,470
is what is sometimes referred
to as a neutral element.

456
00:36:28,470 --> 00:36:48,190
So there exists an element
that we denote by 0.

457
00:36:48,190 --> 00:36:52,290
But it's just to make it
kind of easier to understand,

458
00:36:52,290 --> 00:36:54,330
that we denote by 0.

459
00:36:54,330 --> 00:37:07,770
And it has the property that
if you take this 0 element

460
00:37:07,770 --> 00:37:12,650
and you add it to any
other element, then it just

461
00:37:12,650 --> 00:37:15,250
gives you back
that other element.

462
00:37:15,250 --> 00:37:19,770
So it's kind of neutral
for the operation.

463
00:37:19,770 --> 00:37:24,050
And then there's supposed
to be a fifth property.

464
00:37:24,050 --> 00:37:33,490
And the fifth property is
that for any element x,

465
00:37:33,490 --> 00:37:40,200
there exist another element.

466
00:37:44,650 --> 00:37:49,590
And again, we write this
suggestively by minus x

467
00:37:49,590 --> 00:37:52,950
with a bracket
around, like this.

468
00:37:52,950 --> 00:38:04,526
So there is another
element with the property

469
00:38:04,526 --> 00:38:10,150
that if you take x and you're
adding this other element, then

470
00:38:10,150 --> 00:38:12,750
you get 0.

471
00:38:12,750 --> 00:38:18,990
So those are the five properties
that addition has to have.

472
00:38:18,990 --> 00:38:23,810
And then multiplication have
to have five properties also.

473
00:38:23,810 --> 00:38:29,910
And these are similar to
this with a little twist.

474
00:38:29,910 --> 00:38:36,190
And so for
multiplication, you need

475
00:38:36,190 --> 00:38:37,850
to have the
following properties.

476
00:38:41,950 --> 00:38:44,470
So again, it's five properties.

477
00:38:44,470 --> 00:38:51,170
The first one is the similar
property to the property 1

478
00:38:51,170 --> 00:38:55,370
up there, namely that if you
take two elements, x and y,

479
00:38:55,370 --> 00:39:02,890
in the field, then when
you multiply them together,

480
00:39:02,890 --> 00:39:06,850
that should give you another
element in the field.

481
00:39:06,850 --> 00:39:09,090
So that's the first.

482
00:39:09,090 --> 00:39:16,730
The second one is similar to
2 up there, is that x times y

483
00:39:16,730 --> 00:39:19,350
should be the same as y times x.

484
00:39:22,170 --> 00:39:25,730
So again, that's often
referred to that it

485
00:39:25,730 --> 00:39:30,530
is abelian or commutative.

486
00:39:30,530 --> 00:39:40,010
The third property, it's similar
to the third property up there--

487
00:39:40,010 --> 00:39:43,250
is that if you take
x times y and then

488
00:39:43,250 --> 00:39:46,590
you multiply it by
a third element,

489
00:39:46,590 --> 00:39:50,030
then you could as well
have first multiplied

490
00:39:50,030 --> 00:39:54,990
the two last elements and then
multiply by the first element,

491
00:39:54,990 --> 00:39:57,750
like that.

492
00:39:57,750 --> 00:40:05,510
And then the fourth property
is similar to that property 4

493
00:40:05,510 --> 00:40:22,710
up there, is that there exists
an element denoted suggestively

494
00:40:22,710 --> 00:40:30,390
by 1 here, with a property
that 1 times any element

495
00:40:30,390 --> 00:40:35,790
x just give you back
that element, x.

496
00:40:35,790 --> 00:40:38,890
And then the fifth is
similar, except there

497
00:40:38,890 --> 00:40:40,600
is a twist, slight twist.

498
00:40:44,170 --> 00:40:49,170
And so the fifth
property is that--

499
00:40:49,170 --> 00:40:52,450
but it's very similar to
that fifth over there.

500
00:40:52,450 --> 00:40:54,270
The fifth property
is the following.

501
00:40:57,410 --> 00:41:00,050
The fifth property
here is that there

502
00:41:00,050 --> 00:41:12,170
exists that for any x
in the field except 0--

503
00:41:12,170 --> 00:41:14,970
you have to disregard 0.

504
00:41:14,970 --> 00:41:23,940
For any other elements,
there exist an element--

505
00:41:27,130 --> 00:41:44,270
and, again, suggestively denoted
by 1/x with the property that x

506
00:41:44,270 --> 00:41:48,790
times 1/x is equal to 1.

507
00:41:48,790 --> 00:41:49,290
OK.

508
00:41:49,290 --> 00:41:55,910
So those are the five properties
for addition, five properties

509
00:41:55,910 --> 00:41:57,290
for multiplication.

510
00:41:57,290 --> 00:42:00,950
And you see that the
one for multiplication

511
00:42:00,950 --> 00:42:03,730
is like a mirror of
the one for addition,

512
00:42:03,730 --> 00:42:08,150
except the fifth one has
that you have to look away

513
00:42:08,150 --> 00:42:09,680
from the 0 element.

514
00:42:15,650 --> 00:42:18,310
And so, again, this is just--

515
00:42:18,310 --> 00:42:21,710
1 is just that it
is an operation.

516
00:42:21,710 --> 00:42:25,510
2 is usually called the
abelian property or commutative

517
00:42:25,510 --> 00:42:26,590
property.

518
00:42:26,590 --> 00:42:29,510
This is called the
associative property.

519
00:42:29,510 --> 00:42:34,670
This, it's often also referred
to as a neutral element.

520
00:42:34,670 --> 00:42:40,110
And this here, that
exists this element--

521
00:42:40,110 --> 00:42:43,090
in this case, minus
x is sometimes

522
00:42:43,090 --> 00:42:44,520
called the inverse element--

523
00:42:47,090 --> 00:42:50,130
and likewise for multiplication.

524
00:42:50,130 --> 00:42:54,370
And then there is,
again, an 11th property.

525
00:42:54,370 --> 00:42:55,890
And that's a
property because you

526
00:42:55,890 --> 00:42:58,250
see that those-- at
the moment, there's

527
00:42:58,250 --> 00:43:02,050
basically no interaction
between these two operations.

528
00:43:02,050 --> 00:43:05,050
The only way that you
see some interaction,

529
00:43:05,050 --> 00:43:10,250
but this is very little, is that
the fifth property over here

530
00:43:10,250 --> 00:43:13,570
refer to the neutral
element for addition.

531
00:43:13,570 --> 00:43:17,130
But they don't really chain
the two operations together.

532
00:43:17,130 --> 00:43:20,290
And that is the last property
that is often referred

533
00:43:20,290 --> 00:43:22,070
to as the distributive law.

534
00:43:22,070 --> 00:43:32,800
So this is an axiom
chaining addition--

535
00:43:32,800 --> 00:43:42,230
write it like this, addition
together with multiplication.

536
00:43:53,390 --> 00:44:03,950
And that's if you take x and
you multiply it by y plus z--

537
00:44:03,950 --> 00:44:08,030
you take the sum here, and
then you multiply it by x.

538
00:44:08,030 --> 00:44:11,190
That is supposed to
be the same as where

539
00:44:11,190 --> 00:44:19,830
you first multiply x with y
and then you multiply x by z.

540
00:44:19,830 --> 00:44:23,550
So this is often called
the distributive law.

541
00:44:23,550 --> 00:44:27,390
And that's a law, again, or
axiom that is chaining these two

542
00:44:27,390 --> 00:44:30,303
operations together.

543
00:44:30,303 --> 00:44:30,803
OK.

544
00:44:30,803 --> 00:44:37,610
So now let's try
to see-- so let me

545
00:44:37,610 --> 00:44:43,130
just establish just one
little property of a field.

546
00:44:43,130 --> 00:44:55,382
And you see that when we talk
about the axioms for addition,

547
00:44:55,382 --> 00:44:59,250
I just said that there had
to exist one element that we

548
00:44:59,250 --> 00:45:02,410
call the 0 element, and it
had to have some property.

549
00:45:02,410 --> 00:45:04,270
But maybe there
are many elements.

550
00:45:04,270 --> 00:45:06,080
And so this is the
first little theorem.

551
00:45:10,090 --> 00:45:18,210
For any field-- I just
write it as capital Field.

552
00:45:18,210 --> 00:45:20,460
But it's just to--

553
00:45:20,460 --> 00:45:22,970
but you don't really have to.

554
00:45:22,970 --> 00:45:37,260
For any field F, there
exist only one zero element.

555
00:45:43,390 --> 00:45:44,030
OK.

556
00:45:44,030 --> 00:45:46,750
Let's try to prove this.

557
00:45:46,750 --> 00:45:54,550
And again, there's all
kind of important concepts

558
00:45:54,550 --> 00:45:55,610
in this class.

559
00:45:55,610 --> 00:46:02,910
But there's also another thing
that is important to learn.

560
00:46:02,910 --> 00:46:04,630
And you have to be
a little bit patient

561
00:46:04,630 --> 00:46:09,510
because it will take you a
little bit of time to learn it--

562
00:46:09,510 --> 00:46:13,510
is how to write a
mathematical proof.

563
00:46:13,510 --> 00:46:17,710
And so this here just gives you
just a little bit of idea of it.

564
00:46:17,710 --> 00:46:23,230
So we want to prove that there
exists only one zero element.

565
00:46:23,230 --> 00:46:39,070
So assume that O1 and O2
are both zero element.

566
00:46:45,780 --> 00:46:54,360
So that it's a zero
element just means

567
00:46:54,360 --> 00:46:58,200
that if you take O1 and
you add anything to it--

568
00:46:58,200 --> 00:47:00,440
x could be anything
in the field--

569
00:47:00,440 --> 00:47:02,120
then you get x back.

570
00:47:02,120 --> 00:47:05,080
That is that O1
is a zero element.

571
00:47:05,080 --> 00:47:08,840
That O2 is a zero element
just means the same thing--

572
00:47:08,840 --> 00:47:12,986
that if you take anything
in x, then you get x back.

573
00:47:12,986 --> 00:47:17,520
And now we want to prove
that O1 is equal to O2.

574
00:47:17,520 --> 00:47:21,400
So I just write O1 plus O2.

575
00:47:21,400 --> 00:47:25,800
But now I'm just thinking
about this O2 as my x.

576
00:47:25,800 --> 00:47:30,000
So I'm adding 0 O1 to this x.

577
00:47:30,000 --> 00:47:33,780
So I know that I'm just
getting this here back.

578
00:47:33,780 --> 00:47:38,700
Is because O1 is a 0 element.

579
00:47:38,700 --> 00:47:42,540
But I could do the same
with O2 that I take.

580
00:47:42,540 --> 00:47:46,380
The ordering I know
already is irrelevant,

581
00:47:46,380 --> 00:47:49,160
whether it's O1 plus
O2 or O2 plus O1.

582
00:47:49,160 --> 00:47:50,260
That's the same.

583
00:47:50,260 --> 00:47:54,900
So I can think about
this O1 here as my x.

584
00:47:54,900 --> 00:47:58,880
And so I'm adding the O2 to it.

585
00:47:58,880 --> 00:48:01,660
But then I know that I'm
getting this here back.

586
00:48:01,660 --> 00:48:06,120
So this here must
also be equal to O1.

587
00:48:06,120 --> 00:48:08,210
And so you see that
O1 is equal to O2.

588
00:48:11,940 --> 00:48:18,900
And of course, in the
notes that are post,

589
00:48:18,900 --> 00:48:21,290
things are written
slightly more rigorous.

590
00:48:25,540 --> 00:48:27,540
There's a little
bit more meat to it.

591
00:48:27,540 --> 00:48:31,320
Here, I write it a little
bit shorter on the board.

592
00:48:31,320 --> 00:48:33,790
So look also at the notes.

593
00:48:36,500 --> 00:48:37,000
OK.

594
00:48:39,640 --> 00:48:42,300
So now we know what a field is.

595
00:48:42,300 --> 00:48:46,360
So a field, again, it has
these two operations, additions

596
00:48:46,360 --> 00:48:47,840
and multiplication.

597
00:48:47,840 --> 00:48:50,780
Each of those operations
have five properties.

598
00:48:50,780 --> 00:48:53,800
And then there's 11 properties
that chain those two operations

599
00:48:53,800 --> 00:48:55,920
together.

600
00:48:55,920 --> 00:48:59,470
And so the examples
of a fields is--

601
00:49:04,240 --> 00:49:06,390
so we already have an example.

602
00:49:11,560 --> 00:49:14,290
So the rational numbers
here is a field.

603
00:49:19,200 --> 00:49:26,140
It has these two operations
and with all the 11 properties.

604
00:49:26,140 --> 00:49:33,700
The natural numbers is not
a field, is not a field.

605
00:49:33,700 --> 00:49:38,480
It fails even the
most basic thing.

606
00:49:38,480 --> 00:49:41,240
It fails many of the
properties it needs,

607
00:49:41,240 --> 00:49:44,900
but it fails that even the
serial elements is not in it.

608
00:49:52,260 --> 00:49:57,150
And the integers here
also is not a field.

609
00:50:04,300 --> 00:50:11,160
That is slightly closer to being
a field, but it's still not.

610
00:50:11,160 --> 00:50:13,180
And why is this not a field?

611
00:50:13,180 --> 00:50:15,320
It's for multiplication, right?

612
00:50:15,320 --> 00:50:19,860
If you take 2, then write 2.

613
00:50:19,860 --> 00:50:23,460
If you think about 2,
then there should be--

614
00:50:23,460 --> 00:50:26,580
then the neutral element
for multiplication,

615
00:50:26,580 --> 00:50:29,080
that's the usual 1.

616
00:50:29,080 --> 00:50:31,880
But if you take,
say, the element 2,

617
00:50:31,880 --> 00:50:34,620
then there should be an
element like the inverse.

618
00:50:34,620 --> 00:50:37,920
There should be an element with
the property that if you take 2

619
00:50:37,920 --> 00:50:43,380
and you multiply with that
element, then you should get 1.

620
00:50:43,380 --> 00:50:46,060
That should be the inverse
element, 1 over it.

621
00:50:46,060 --> 00:50:47,580
But this is like a half.

622
00:50:47,580 --> 00:50:51,860
But you're missing a
half in the integers.

623
00:50:51,860 --> 00:50:53,740
So that's why this
here is not a field.

624
00:50:56,480 --> 00:50:57,770
OK.

625
00:50:57,770 --> 00:51:02,200
So the next concept
is-- so, again, we're

626
00:51:02,200 --> 00:51:06,200
working our way towards
trying to understand

627
00:51:06,200 --> 00:51:08,720
what property of
the real numbers

628
00:51:08,720 --> 00:51:13,040
that we need in order to
make the intermediate value

629
00:51:13,040 --> 00:51:18,560
theorem, the proof regression.

630
00:51:18,560 --> 00:51:24,600
And so we now know
what a field is.

631
00:51:29,420 --> 00:51:34,620
The next few slides, I'll talk
about complete, what that means.

632
00:51:34,620 --> 00:51:40,440
But we also have this ordering.

633
00:51:40,440 --> 00:51:43,420
So what does it mean
that a field is ordered?

634
00:51:43,420 --> 00:51:48,680
So let's start with
an ordered set.

635
00:51:53,940 --> 00:51:55,740
So an ordered set--

636
00:51:55,740 --> 00:52:03,820
so this is a set
denoted by capital S.

637
00:52:03,820 --> 00:52:07,300
And it has the property that
whenever you have two elements

638
00:52:07,300 --> 00:52:12,780
in the set, then it should
either be-- and the ordering,

639
00:52:12,780 --> 00:52:17,280
by the way-- so an ordered
set with an ordering--

640
00:52:23,120 --> 00:52:27,060
and I denote the ordering,
again, suggestively by this.

641
00:52:27,060 --> 00:52:28,660
I could have used
any other symbol,

642
00:52:28,660 --> 00:52:31,360
but I'm just suggestively
using this symbol,

643
00:52:31,360 --> 00:52:35,380
just to make it easier
to remember the property.

644
00:52:35,380 --> 00:52:39,600
It's like this property
that you know so well.

645
00:52:39,600 --> 00:52:46,380
So an ordered set is a
set with an ordering--

646
00:52:52,760 --> 00:52:55,400
and, again, denoted by this--

647
00:52:55,400 --> 00:53:00,600
so that if you take any
two elements in the set,

648
00:53:00,600 --> 00:53:02,830
then one of the three
properties should hold.

649
00:53:07,040 --> 00:53:09,140
The following three
properties should hold.

650
00:53:26,060 --> 00:53:33,380
One is that-- so either
x should be equal to y.

651
00:53:33,380 --> 00:53:38,460
2, the other possibility,
is that x is smaller than y.

652
00:53:38,460 --> 00:53:43,740
Or 3, y is smaller than x.

653
00:53:43,740 --> 00:53:46,560
So basically, if you
have an ordered set,

654
00:53:46,560 --> 00:53:48,300
whenever you take
two elements, you

655
00:53:48,300 --> 00:53:51,180
should be able to compare
these two elements.

656
00:53:51,180 --> 00:53:51,680
Of course.

657
00:53:51,680 --> 00:53:54,340
These two elements could
be the same elements.

658
00:53:54,340 --> 00:53:56,020
But if they're not
the same element,

659
00:53:56,020 --> 00:54:00,340
then one should be
smaller than the other.

660
00:54:00,340 --> 00:54:01,310
So that's the idea.

661
00:54:03,860 --> 00:54:05,000
OK.

662
00:54:05,000 --> 00:54:10,410
And so now the next concept
is an ordered field.

663
00:54:15,480 --> 00:54:22,220
So now we have a field,
and we have an ordered set.

664
00:54:22,220 --> 00:54:46,310
So an ordered field is a field
that is also an ordered set.

665
00:54:51,240 --> 00:54:56,720
And then the operations from
the fields and the ordering,

666
00:54:56,720 --> 00:54:58,860
they should speak to each other.

667
00:54:58,860 --> 00:55:01,320
So there should be an
interaction between these two

668
00:55:01,320 --> 00:55:03,200
operations.

669
00:55:03,200 --> 00:55:17,940
So it has to have-- with
the following properties,

670
00:55:17,940 --> 00:55:19,679
then if you take--

671
00:55:23,060 --> 00:55:24,900
so it's following
two properties,

672
00:55:24,900 --> 00:55:35,000
that if x is less than y and
you're adding a c to both--

673
00:55:35,000 --> 00:55:45,140
so this here implies that
for any z in the field,

674
00:55:45,140 --> 00:55:47,800
if you add this
z to both sides--

675
00:55:47,800 --> 00:55:53,620
so x plus z is
less than y plus z.

676
00:55:53,620 --> 00:55:58,600
So you should be able to
add something to both sides

677
00:55:58,600 --> 00:56:03,340
and it's still less
than, like that.

678
00:56:06,260 --> 00:56:09,620
So this here is,
how do the ordering

679
00:56:09,620 --> 00:56:11,780
interacts with addition?

680
00:56:11,780 --> 00:56:15,160
And the other thing is,
how do it act interacts

681
00:56:15,160 --> 00:56:17,800
with multiplication?

682
00:56:17,800 --> 00:56:22,440
And there, the property is
that if you take a x that

683
00:56:22,440 --> 00:56:29,440
is positive and y is positive--

684
00:56:29,440 --> 00:56:30,480
and the y.

685
00:56:30,480 --> 00:56:33,920
So you have both x and
y that are positive.

686
00:56:33,920 --> 00:56:39,480
Then the product of these
two should also be positive.

687
00:56:39,480 --> 00:56:42,960
So then this here, the
product of the two,

688
00:56:42,960 --> 00:56:44,560
should also be positive.

689
00:56:44,560 --> 00:56:51,060
And so this is how the ordering
interact with multiplication.

690
00:56:51,060 --> 00:56:53,720
This is how it
interacts with addition.

691
00:56:53,720 --> 00:56:56,100
This is how it interacts
with multiplication.

692
00:57:00,590 --> 00:57:07,360
And so now-- well, the
first thing you observe

693
00:57:07,360 --> 00:57:11,840
is that, again, you already
know an example of this.

694
00:57:15,540 --> 00:57:24,520
So the rational numbers--

695
00:57:24,520 --> 00:57:28,230
so Q here is an ordered field.

696
00:57:36,300 --> 00:57:39,580
We have already seen
that it is a field,

697
00:57:39,580 --> 00:57:41,120
has those 11 properties.

698
00:57:41,120 --> 00:57:43,860
It has addition, multiplication.

699
00:57:43,860 --> 00:57:45,480
Each of those have 5 property.

700
00:57:45,480 --> 00:57:49,380
It has the 11 that
chain the two together.

701
00:57:49,380 --> 00:57:51,940
And we also discussed
the ordering.

702
00:57:51,940 --> 00:57:53,960
If you take two
rational numbers,

703
00:57:53,960 --> 00:57:56,240
what does it mean that one
is less than the other?

704
00:57:56,240 --> 00:57:58,620
It has the property
of an ordering,

705
00:57:58,620 --> 00:58:02,340
that you can compare any
two elements, any two

706
00:58:02,340 --> 00:58:05,240
rational numbers, that
either they are the same

707
00:58:05,240 --> 00:58:08,360
or, if you have two, one
is less than the other.

708
00:58:14,040 --> 00:58:18,480
And it also has this,
the additive property.

709
00:58:18,480 --> 00:58:23,400
And also, the ordering interact
with addition in this way.

710
00:58:23,400 --> 00:58:26,600
And it interacts with
multiplication in this way.

711
00:58:26,600 --> 00:58:33,280
So the rational number is
indeed an ordered field.

712
00:58:33,280 --> 00:58:39,360
Now let me just establish
one last property.

713
00:58:39,360 --> 00:58:42,670
And that is that if I take--

714
00:58:47,880 --> 00:58:50,180
well, let me try to prove
a little theorem here.

715
00:58:57,840 --> 00:59:03,110
So here's the theorem.

716
00:59:09,420 --> 00:59:15,420
So F here is supposed
to be an ordered field.

717
00:59:20,500 --> 00:59:25,940
And then we have that
x here is less than y.

718
00:59:25,940 --> 00:59:34,260
And z here, this is positive.

719
00:59:34,260 --> 00:59:36,820
So I assume I have this.

720
00:59:36,820 --> 00:59:40,060
And now I want to show
that-- then I claim that

721
00:59:40,060 --> 00:59:43,500
from these properties that
our ordered field have,

722
00:59:43,500 --> 00:59:51,580
I get automatically then x
times c is less than y times c.

723
00:59:51,580 --> 00:59:55,700
So I claim I get
this property here

724
00:59:55,700 --> 00:59:59,580
that you know these
properties over here.

725
00:59:59,580 --> 01:00:01,720
So let's try to prove that.

726
01:00:19,310 --> 01:00:23,550
So what I want to do is--

727
01:00:23,550 --> 01:00:35,890
so we want to show that x
times z is less than y times z.

728
01:00:35,890 --> 01:00:38,350
This is what we want to show.

729
01:00:38,350 --> 01:00:42,090
Now, to say this here--

730
01:00:42,090 --> 01:00:58,050
this here is equivalent
to showing that--

731
01:00:58,050 --> 01:01:01,030
so if you take, this you could--

732
01:01:01,030 --> 01:01:09,890
because this here, if you
add minus y times z-- sorry.

733
01:01:12,570 --> 01:01:17,470
If you take this thing
here and to both sides--

734
01:01:17,470 --> 01:01:26,890
so you take x times z and
then you plus minus x times z,

735
01:01:26,890 --> 01:01:31,830
then this here is the same as--

736
01:01:31,830 --> 01:01:35,490
to say that this is less than
that is equivalent to saying

737
01:01:35,490 --> 01:01:36,430
this thing here.

738
01:01:41,530 --> 01:01:43,810
Why is that equivalent?

739
01:01:43,810 --> 01:01:48,530
This is this law here that is
chaining the ordering together

740
01:01:48,530 --> 01:01:50,970
with addition.

741
01:01:50,970 --> 01:01:56,310
And this here,
this element here,

742
01:01:56,310 --> 01:02:00,510
this is just the inverse
element of this product here.

743
01:02:04,679 --> 01:02:08,710
But now you see that
these two things together,

744
01:02:08,710 --> 01:02:10,082
that just gives you 0.

745
01:02:19,030 --> 01:02:26,950
This thing here, this
here is the same as x.

746
01:02:26,950 --> 01:02:30,790
So maybe we need to show this.

747
01:02:30,790 --> 01:02:32,790
But this here, it's
not hard to believe

748
01:02:32,790 --> 01:02:43,990
that this here is the same
as y times z minus x times z.

749
01:02:43,990 --> 01:02:46,850
So this here is now--
you can factor out.

750
01:02:46,850 --> 01:02:51,910
So this is this law, the 11th
law that allow you to factor

751
01:02:51,910 --> 01:02:53,070
this one out.

752
01:02:53,070 --> 01:02:58,350
So this is the same
as y minus x times z.

753
01:02:58,350 --> 01:03:04,450
This was the distributive
law and was the 11th law.

754
01:03:04,450 --> 01:03:10,170
But now you see that because
y was bigger than x--

755
01:03:10,170 --> 01:03:20,210
since y here was
bigger than x, this

756
01:03:20,210 --> 01:03:23,850
is equivalent to saying
that y is bigger than x--

757
01:03:23,850 --> 01:03:28,450
if you subtract x
on both sides, this

758
01:03:28,450 --> 01:03:34,090
is equivalent to saying that
y minus x is bigger than 0.

759
01:03:34,090 --> 01:03:40,520
This is, again, using the
first axiom over here.

760
01:03:40,520 --> 01:03:44,150
And so you see that
now we have that.

761
01:03:44,150 --> 01:03:49,810
So we want to prove that
this thing here, this

762
01:03:49,810 --> 01:03:53,410
here is all equivalent to
proving that this thing here

763
01:03:53,410 --> 01:03:55,890
is bigger than 0.

764
01:03:55,890 --> 01:03:58,750
So the claim up there
is equivalent to proving

765
01:03:58,750 --> 01:04:02,390
that this thing here
is bigger than 0.

766
01:04:02,390 --> 01:04:07,070
But the difference here, this
difference here is positive.

767
01:04:07,070 --> 01:04:09,590
This is assumed to be positive.

768
01:04:09,590 --> 01:04:13,590
The second law that is
chaining the ordering together

769
01:04:13,590 --> 01:04:17,390
with multiplication say that
if you have something positive

770
01:04:17,390 --> 01:04:19,330
and you multiply it
by something positive,

771
01:04:19,330 --> 01:04:21,230
you're getting
something positive.

772
01:04:21,230 --> 01:04:24,550
And so in order to
prove this here,

773
01:04:24,550 --> 01:04:27,870
it was the same as proving
that this thing here

774
01:04:27,870 --> 01:04:29,670
is actually positive.

775
01:04:29,670 --> 01:04:35,450
And we have now just reduced
it to this second axiom.

776
01:04:35,450 --> 01:04:35,950
OK.

777
01:04:38,630 --> 01:04:41,710
Is that clear?

778
01:04:41,710 --> 01:04:43,010
OK.

779
01:04:43,010 --> 01:04:44,910
So we go just a
little bit easier

780
01:04:44,910 --> 01:04:46,130
here in the first lecture.

781
01:04:46,130 --> 01:04:49,990
This is what I have
for the first lecture.

782
01:04:49,990 --> 01:04:53,750
But there's a bunch
of different concepts.

